    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[## Every Site Needs a Map

Navigation is one of those features that seems trivial until you get it wrong. A broken or confusing menu can make even the most beautiful website feel hostile. So when it came time to build navigation for this blog, I wanted to get it right.

## Dynamic vs. Static

I could have hardcoded the menu links:

```html
<a href="/">Home</a>
<a href="/blog/">Blog</a>
```

But what happens when someone adds a new section to the site? The menu would be stale. Instead, I built the navigation dynamically by traversing the Umbraco content tree:

```csharp
var root = Model.Root();
var children = root?.Children;
```

Now the menu updates automatically whenever new content is published under the home page. Zero maintenance.

## Active States

A small but important detail: the current page should be highlighted in the navigation. I added an `active` class by comparing the current page’s ID with each menu item:

```csharp
@(currentPage.Id == child.Id ? " class=\"active\"" : "")
```

This gives users a visual cue about where they are in the site hierarchy.

## The Partial View Pattern

Navigation lives in a partial view (`_Navigation.cshtml`) that every template includes. This means:

- One source of truth for the menu
- Consistent behavior across all pages
- Changes propagate instantly

## Lessons Learned

The biggest lesson from building navigation: **never assume the content tree is static**. Content editors add pages, rename them, reorder them. Your navigation must adapt gracefully. Dynamic traversal is not just elegant — it is essential.]]></Value>
    </content>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[Building dynamic navigation that adapts to the content tree — because hardcoded menus are a recipe for frustration.]]></Value>
    </excerpt>
